    "description": "Nyoom — Breaking CSP with CSS and ESI — Nyoom is a small profile-sharing application behind a Varnish reverse proxy. Users can register, edit their bio, and report a dashboard URL for an admin bot to visit. The challenge is to recover the admin session, which is also the flag.\n\nA straightforward stored XSS payload is blocked by a nonce-ba",
    "problemDescription": "## Overview\n\nNyoom is a small profile-sharing application behind a Varnish reverse proxy. Users can register, edit their bio, and report a dashboard URL for an admin bot to visit. The challenge is to recover the admin session, which is also the flag.\n\nA straightforward stored XSS payload is blocked by a nonce-based Content Security Policy (CSP). Direct attempts to read the session also fail: the session cookie is \\`HttpOnly\\`, and Varnish rejects top-level access to \\`/fragments/session-info\\`.\n\nThe solution chains **four behaviors**:\n\n1. **Stored HTML injection:** a profile bio is inserted into the dashboard as HTML, with no effective HTML sanitization.\n2. **CSS-based nonce disclosure:** an attacker-controlled stylesheet matches substrings of the nonce exposed in a CSP \\`<meta>\\` element.\n3. **Stylesheet-gated race:** a second stylesheet remains pending until the solver updates the profile, so the legitimate nonce-authorized script fetches the updated bio on the *same document*.\n4. **ESI session disclosure:** the resulting JavaScript submits an ESI include to \\`/preview\\`; Varnish expands it using the admin's cookies, exposing the session in the response.",
